`,s.jsx(I,{items:[{q:"One condition decides Z-test versus t-test. Which, and why does it matter?",a:"Whether sigma is KNOWN from outside the data or estimated from the sample. If estimated, the denominator is itself random, adding uncertainty the standard normal does not account for — so the statistic has heavier tails and follows Student's t. Using Z with an estimated sigma gives p-values that are too small, especially at small n."},{q:"Distinguish the standard deviation from the standard error in one sentence each.",a:"The standard deviation describes how much individual observations vary around the mean. The standard error describes how much the SAMPLE MEAN varies from sample to sample, and equals sigma over root n — so it shrinks as you collect more data while the standard deviation does not."},{q:"The population of box weights is skewed. Can you still run a Z-test?",a:"Yes, if n is large enough — roughly 30 or more. The test needs the SAMPLING DISTRIBUTION OF THE MEAN to be normal, not the population, and the Central Limit Theorem delivers that regardless of population shape. The heavier the skew, the larger the n required."},{q:"x-bar = 497, mu0 = 500, sigma = 8, n = 40. Compute Z and interpret its size.",a:"SE = 8/sqrt(40) = 1.265, so Z = -3/1.265 = -2.37. The sample mean sits 2.37 standard errors below target; a departure that large or larger occurs about 1.8% of the time in either direction if the line were correctly calibrated."},{q:"The same data give p = 0.0456: reject at alpha 0.05, fail to reject at 0.01. What does that teach?",a:"That the reject/fail dichotomy is a convention laid on top of a continuous measure, not a fact read from the data. It is also why alpha must be fixed before the analysis — otherwise the threshold can be chosen after the fact to produce whichever verdict is wanted."}]}),`
